1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:13,248
Let's understand Conditional Co-Ablation — a method for finding the hidden backup parts of a neural network's reasoning. We'll build it up from the very beginning, assuming no background in interpretability.

2
00:00:13,588 --> 00:00:22,276
Along the way we'll see something uncomfortable: the standard test we use to check a circuit can itself change the circuit we're testing.

3
00:00:23,026 --> 00:00:28,954
First, what are we even looking at? A language model reads text and predicts the next word.

4
00:00:29,294 --> 00:00:39,254
Mechanistic interpretability is the effort to reverse-engineer how it does that — not just what it answers, but the computation that produces the answer.

5
00:00:39,594 --> 00:00:50,610
The model is built from small, repeated parts. The ones we'll focus on are attention heads — each a tiny unit that moves and mixes information between words.

6
00:00:50,950 --> 00:01:02,590
For any one task, only a handful of these heads actually do the work. That small, connected set of components is called a circuit — the model's wiring for a single behavior.

7
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:09,220
How do researchers find a circuit? With a simple, powerful tool: ablation.

8
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:18,872
You switch off one component and watch how much the answer changes. A big drop in the correct answer means it mattered; a small drop means it didn't.

9
00:01:19,212 --> 00:01:28,236
Do this for every head, rank them by effect, and the important ones light up. This single-ablation score is the workhorse of the field.

10
00:01:28,576 --> 00:01:42,904
Here's a concrete circuit. Given “Mary and John went to the store; John gave a drink to blank,” the model answers “Mary.” Three name-mover heads carry that name to the output. Ablate them, and the model should fail.

11
00:01:43,654 --> 00:01:49,702
But something strange happens. You delete those three heads — and the answer barely changes.

12
00:01:50,042 --> 00:02:00,962
The reason is self-repair. The moment a key component is removed, another component that was sitting silent wakes up, takes over its job, and re-routes the answer.

13
00:02:01,302 --> 00:02:11,574
This is redundancy. The network keeps dormant backups and heals itself under damage — a little like a power grid rerouting around a failed line.

14
00:02:11,914 --> 00:02:22,882
In this circuit there are eight documented backup heads. Delete the three primaries and the answer margin moves by only zero point two eight, out of two point five seven.

15
00:02:23,222 --> 00:02:38,342
Now watch what that does to our measurement. The primaries look unimportant, because the backups hid the damage. And the backups look unimportant too, because on the intact model they were silent. One measurement, two wrong conclusions.

16
00:02:39,092 --> 00:02:49,748
And here is the sharper version of the problem. Ablation isn't a neutral observation — it's an intervention that changes which circuit is carrying the behavior.

17
00:02:50,088 --> 00:02:58,104
So a circuit can faithfully explain the original model, and still be incomplete under the very ablation we used to test it.

18
00:02:58,444 --> 00:03:09,604
You can measure that non-additivity directly. Ablating the primaries together with their backups does one point nine times as much damage as the sum of their separate effects.

19
00:03:09,944 --> 00:03:21,248
That raises a conditional question. Which components become causally important only after a known part of the circuit is removed? We call answering it conditional circuit completion.

20
00:03:21,998 --> 00:03:26,726
So CoAx changes the question. Don't ask how important a unit is on its own.

21
00:03:27,066 --> 00:03:34,506
Ask a conditional one: once a supplied primary set has already been removed, how much does this unit's effect grow?

22
00:03:34,846 --> 00:03:47,398
A true backup does almost nothing by itself — its effect alone is near zero. But once its partner is gone, its effect becomes large. That growth is the CoAx score.

23
00:03:47,738 --> 00:03:59,210
It needs no labels and no gradients — only forward passes. Silent alone, load-bearing once its primary is gone: that is exactly the signature of a backup.

24
00:03:59,960 --> 00:04:05,216
Two results say this is the right instrument, not just a clever heuristic.

25
00:04:05,556 --> 00:04:22,596
The first is a limitation proof. A perfectly dormant backup can be mathematically indistinguishable from a completely irrelevant component — for every score that only looks at the intact model. Not just for weak scores; for the entire class.

26
00:04:22,936 --> 00:04:37,888
The second says what conditioning actually buys. The change in a component's ablation effect, once the primary set is removed, is exactly the sum of every interaction — pairs, triples, and higher — linking it to what you removed.

27
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:50,660
And you can see that structure. Measure the interaction between every pair of heads, and the primaries and their backups light up as a bright block — a module a one-head-at-a-time score can never see.

28
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:59,688
Enumerating all those terms would be combinatorial. Conditioning hands you the whole aggregate at once, one forward pass per candidate.

29
00:05:00,438 --> 00:05:07,854
Now, the evidence. We start where we actually have ground truth — the circuit with eight documented backup heads.

30
00:05:08,194 --> 00:05:20,410
We supply the three primaries, rank the other one hundred and forty-one heads, and check how well the known backups rise to the top. One is perfect; a half is random guessing.

31
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:30,134
Scores that only read the intact model land between zero point six and zero point eight two, even the gradient methods designed to be smarter.

32
00:05:30,474 --> 00:05:42,426
You might think reading the model after the ablation is enough. That gets zero point seven six — better, but it still mixes importance a head already had with importance it newly gained.

33
00:05:42,766 --> 00:05:54,862
CoAx compares the two states, and reaches zero point nine four one. The gain isn't a smarter score; it's asking which heads become important because the primary set was removed.

34
00:05:55,612 --> 00:06:05,932
Before believing that, we should ask a suspicious question. Maybe any big enough intervention would surface these heads, and the specific circuit doesn't matter.

35
00:06:06,272 --> 00:06:19,784
So we test it hard. We search five thousand random three-head sets and keep the sixteen that best match the true primaries — in how much they damage behavior, in how far they move the output, and in depth.

36
00:06:20,124 --> 00:06:33,948
Those matched impostors recover the backups at only about zero point four — while the true primary set stays at zero point nine four. Recovery tracks which circuit was removed, not how hard the model was hit.

37
00:06:34,288 --> 00:06:50,608
One more suspicion: ablation also disturbs the model's normalization. But replaying the ablated pass with normalization held fixed pushes recovery up to zero point nine eight three — so normalization isn't creating the signal.

38
00:06:51,358 --> 00:06:56,806
A high score still isn't proof. Are these heads really backups? We test them causally.

39
00:06:57,146 --> 00:07:07,442
As we remove the primaries one by one, the heads CoAx found wake up — their output grows and they start writing the correct answer — while matched random heads stay flat.

40
00:07:07,782 --> 00:07:19,518
The documented backup branch roughly triples its contribution to the answer, from plus zero point five seven to plus one point six eight, while the answer margin barely falls.

41
00:07:19,858 --> 00:07:39,850
Then the decisive test: freeze the eight heads CoAx picked so they cannot respond, and the answer margin drops by zero point nine — against zero point two seven for a random set of the same size, and essentially nothing for the matched conditional control. They don't just correlate with the healing; they carry it.

42
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:46,864
Finding the backups isn't the real goal. The goal is to repair the explanation that was incomplete.

43
00:07:47,204 --> 00:08:04,316
Here's the test. Take the documented circuit, delete its backup branch, and measure how badly it now fails to reproduce the full model's response. That incompleteness is zero point seven five; the complete circuit sits at zero point two eight.

44
00:08:04,656 --> 00:08:17,688
Now add the eight heads CoAx found — using the same ranking as before, with no labels, no gradients, and no task-specific tuning. Incompleteness falls to zero point two one.

45
00:08:18,028 --> 00:08:35,644
And it works in reverse. Remove those heads instead, and the damage closely reproduces what removing the documented backups does, while barely touching unrelated text. The recovered set doesn't just rank well — it plays the missing branch's causal role.

46
00:08:36,394 --> 00:08:42,826
Is this one lucky circuit? We test the conditional signal where no backup labels exist at all.

47
00:08:43,166 --> 00:09:01,094
First, across mechanisms. We build a label-free causal target — freeze a candidate after the ablation and see how much extra behavior is lost. Conditional growth agrees with it in eleven of twelve held-out cases, across four mechanism families.

48
00:09:01,434 --> 00:09:18,282
Second, across models. On eight non-GPT-2 models spanning six architecture families, CoAx-selected completions beat a size-matched random set every single time — and beat each model's own next-best heads in six of the eight.

49
00:09:19,032 --> 00:09:29,208
Step back, and the lesson is about measurement itself. A component's importance is not a fixed property — it is conditional on what else is still present.

50
00:09:29,548 --> 00:09:38,284
The parts that matter most are often the ones that stay silent until they are needed. Measure them in isolation, and you will miss them every time.

51
00:09:38,624 --> 00:09:54,032
So a causal explanation of a self-repairing model isn't a single circuit. It's an account of how responsibility reorganizes when that circuit is disrupted — and CoAx recovers exactly the part it reorganizes onto.

52
00:09:54,372 --> 00:10:00,324
The code, the paper, and an interactive page are linked on screen. Thanks for watching.

